Modern HPC systems generate massive heterogeneous telemetry data streams with hundreds of metrics per node sampled at high frequencies across thousands of nodes, presenting fundamental challenges in storage, integration, and real-time analysis. This paper presents Mantis, a scalable framework for telemetry data management that automatically learns system behavior from unlabeled data streams. Mantis introduces the SysMixer Model, an unsupervised learning approach that discovers cross-metric correlations and temporal dependencies. The model generates Telemetry Relation Graphs (TRGs) that encode learned relationships, enabling predictive queries over future system states and data-driven discovery of hardware dependencies. We evaluate Mantis on production telemetry datasets from two HPC clusters. Mantis operates without labeled data or application-specific configuration. By leveraging TRG dynamics to distinguish workload transitions from true anomalies, Mantis reduces false positive rates by 26 × , 12 × , and 3 × compared to VAE, GAT, and GDN respectively, while detecting real GPU failures on OLCF with 10-minute early warning.